==English==

===Etymology===
Partly from {{inh|en|enm|discleymer}}, from {{der|en|xno|desclamer}}; and partly from {{af|en|disclaim|-er|id2=agent noun}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/dɪsˈkleɪm.ɚ/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/dɪsˈkleɪm.ə/}}
* {{hyph|en|dis|claim|er}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-disclaimer.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪmə(ɹ)|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun}}

# One who disclaims, disowns, or renounces.
# A public [[disavowal]], as of responsibility, pretensions, claims, opinions, etc.
# {{lb|en|legal}} A denial, disavowal, or renunciation, as of a title, claim, interest, estate, or trust; relinquishment or waiver of an interest or estate.
# {{lb|en|proscribed}} A [[disclosure]] of an interest, relationship, or the like.
#* {{quote-journal|en|May 10 2012|Anant Rangaswami|No need for regulation in media – it’s happening by itself|Firstpost|text=It interviewed, among others, the director of Vasant Valley School, owned by the same family that part-owns ''Mail Today''. No '''disclaimer''' was carried stating as much.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|April 23 2018|Hallie Detrick|What We Know About Sean Hannity's Shell Companies and Why It Matters|Fortune|text=Though the fact that the two men do business together was disclosed on air, a recent op-ed penned by Lako and published on the Hannity show’s website had no such '''disclaimer'''.}}

====Usage notes====
* The use in the sense of a disclosure rather than a disavowal is a recent extension in meaning that may be considered incorrect usage.

